  data: {
    LLM: "Prioritize your 'Dui' (True Pairs). Because they give you priority to jump the turn order, having two identical cards in hand is a major defensive move—you can 'Steal' the turn from your opponents at any time. Avoid holding 'Ka' (False Pairs) if you can, as they are easily blocked by players holding the matching Dui cards.\n\nHand Thinning: Focus your collection on one or two suits. Since there are only 4 of each card in the 120-card deck, if you hold two identical 5-Strings, the probability of someone else having the other two is manageable. If you spread across all three suits, you are less likely to stumble upon the specific cards needed for a Dui.\n\nDiscard Baiting: If you have a 'Dui' of 4-Coins and a singleton 5-Coins, discard the 5. This signals to opponents that you aren't interested in that range, potentially inducing them to discard their 4-Coins later in the round. This 'Social Engineering' of the discard pile is common among veteran Quan Dui gamblers.",
    Player: "Prioritize your 'Dui' (True Pairs). Because they give you priority to jump the turn order, having two identical cards in hand is a major defensive move—you can 'Steal' the turn from your opponents at any time. Avoid holding 'Ka' (False Pairs) if you can, as they are easily blocked by players holding the matching Dui cards.\n\nHand Thinning: Focus your collection on one or two suits. Since there are only 4 of each card in the 120-card deck, if you hold two identical 5-Strings, the probability of someone else having the other two is manageable. If you spread across all three suits, you are less likely to stumble upon the specific cards needed for a Dui.\n\nDiscard Baiting: If you have a 'Dui' of 4-Coins and a singleton 5-Coins, discard the 5. This signals to opponents that you aren't interested in that range, potentially inducing them to discard their 4-Coins later in the round. This 'Social Engineering' of the discard pile is common among veteran Quan Dui gamblers.",
